---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import Used from "../components/Used.astro";
import { headingDisplayTree, headingDocumentTree, textScaleTree, textTitleTree } from "../demos/typography";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../i18n";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

/* One row per size role. The resolved values are read live from the page. */
const pairs = [
  { role: "display-lg", size: "--font-size-display-lg", leading: "--font-line-height-display-lg", note: "Heroes and covers" },
  { role: "display-md", size: "--font-size-display-md", leading: "--font-line-height-display-md", note: "Heroes and covers" },
  { role: "display-sm", size: "--font-size-display-sm", leading: "--font-line-height-display-sm", note: "A product page title" },
  { role: "h1", size: "--font-size-heading-h1", leading: "--font-line-height-heading-h1", note: "Document title" },
  { role: "h2", size: "--font-size-heading-h2", leading: "--font-line-height-heading-h2", note: "Section" },
  { role: "h3", size: "--font-size-heading-h3", leading: "--font-line-height-heading-h3", note: "Subsection" },
  { role: "h4 · h5 · h6", size: "--font-size-heading-h4", leading: "--font-line-height-heading-h4", note: "Floor: it stops shrinking here" },
  { role: "body-lg", size: "--font-size-body-lg", leading: "--font-line-height-body-lg", note: "Subtitle under a heading" },
  { role: "body", size: "--font-size-body", leading: "--font-line-height-body", note: "Reading text, the default" },
  { role: "body-sm", size: "--font-size-body-sm", leading: "--font-line-height-body-sm", note: "Secondary text, dense cells" },
  { role: "caption", size: "--font-size-caption", leading: "--font-line-height-caption", note: "Metadata, captions, eyebrows" },
];

const weights = [
  { token: "--font-weight-body", value: "400", note: "Reading text" },
  { token: "--font-weight-label", value: "500", note: "Control and navigation labels" },
  { token: "--font-weight-emphasis", value: "600", note: "<strong> inside a paragraph, eyebrows" },
  { token: "--font-weight-heading", value: "700", note: "Headings and displays" },
];

const install = `/* Once, at the app root, before the tokens */
@import "@skryensya/core/fonts/hanken-grotesk.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";`;

const swap = `/* Another family: skip hanken-grotesk.css and redefine the primitive */
:root {
  --scale-font-family-sans: "My Family", system-ui, sans-serif;
}`;

const usage = `<!-- Document level and visual size are separate -->
<h2 class="sk-heading" data-size="h3">Regions ready</h2>
<p class="sk-text" data-tone="secondary">Three of four regions responded.</p>

<!-- Your own component reads roles, never pixels -->
<style>
  .my-card__title {
    font-size: var(--font-size-heading-h4);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-heading-h4);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-heading);
  }
</style>`;
---

<Base
  title="Typography"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="The system's type scale: families, size and line-height pairs, one weight per role, and how to consume them."
>
  <h1>Typography</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A sans family that ships with the package, a mono for code, eleven size roles that carry their
    own line-height, and four named weights. No component writes a <code>font-size</code> in pixels:
    each reads a semantic token from <code>packages/core/css/semantic/_type.scss</code>, which in turn
    points at the primitive scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Families</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hanken Grotesk</strong> is the system's sans, and it ships inside
    <code>@skryensya/core</code>: nothing is fetched from someone else's CDN. It is variable (one weight
    axis from 100 to 900), so the four weights the system uses are drawn, never synthesised, and it has
    real tabular figures for tables, meters and clocks. It was picked by measuring this site's own
    pages: its x-height holds at 12px, the size of the densest chrome, and it is the most contained of
    the candidates at 60px.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>JetBrains Mono</strong> is the code family. The package does not include it: when it is not
    installed, the operating system's monospace takes over.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Token</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Primitive</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Resolved value</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--font-family-body</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--scale-font-family-sans</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono"><Used token="--font-family-body" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--font-family-code</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--scale-font-family-mono</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono"><Used token="--font-family-code" /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The font sheet also declares a fallback with Hanken Grotesk's metrics: the reader's local system
    font, rescaled to the real face's width and line box. With <code>font-display: swap</code> the first
    paint uses that fallback, and the swap to the real font changes the drawing of the letters without
    moving the layout.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="CSS" />
  <CodeBlock code={swap} label="CSS" note="swapping the family" />

  <h2>The scale: size and line-height come in pairs</h2>
  <p>
    Every size role has a paired line-height, and the leading tightens as the size grows: 1.5 for
    reading, 1.35 for small headings, 1.2 for h1 and 1.1 for displays. A 60px display with a
    paragraph's leading leaves wide gaps between lines. That is why the two columns are read together:
    <code>--font-size-heading-h2</code> always goes with <code>--font-line-height-heading-h2</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Role</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Size</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Line-height</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">When</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        {
          pairs.map((pair) => (
            <tr class="sk-table__row">
              <td class="sk-table__cell"><strong>{pair.role}</strong></td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{pair.size}<br /><Used token={pair.size} /></td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{pair.leading}<br /><Used token={pair.leading} /></td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{pair.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Displays</h3>
  <p>
    Above the document scale, for heroes and covers. They map to no heading level: a display can be an
    <code>h1</code> or a <code>p</code>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingDisplayTree(t)} label="Displays" />

  <h3>Document headings</h3>
  <p>
    Each rung from h1 to h4 is visible. h5 and h6 reuse the h4 pair: below 18px a section title stops
    standing out from the text it introduces.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingDocumentTree(t)} label="h1 to h6" />

  <h3>Reading text</h3>
  <p>
    Four body sizes, all at 1.5 leading. Long-form text can also use
    <code>--font-line-height-body-relaxed</code> (1.7), applied on purpose, not by default.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={textScaleTree()} label="caption to lg" />

  <h2>Weights: one per role</h2>
  <p>
    Four rungs, none repeated. <code>emphasis</code> and <code>label</code> used to share a value (500),
    so a <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> in a paragraph read exactly like a button label. Now each role has
    its own weight and the whole 400 to 700 ladder is in use.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Token</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Weight</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">When</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        {
          weights.map((weight) => (
            <tr class="sk-table__row">
              <td class="sk-table__cell mono">{weight.token}</td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell"><span style={`font-weight: var(${weight.token})`}>{weight.value}</span></td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{weight.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Composed roles</h2>
  <p>
    Some jobs repeat in every title block and combine several axes at once.
    <a href="/components/text">Text</a> publishes them as roles: <code>eyebrow</code> (caption, emphasis
    weight, tracked uppercase) above the title, and <code>subtitle</code> (body-lg, relaxed leading,
    secondary tone) below it. Every hook a role sets can still be overridden.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={textTitleTree(t)} label="Eyebrow, title and subtitle" />

  <h2>Usage</h2>
  <p>
    For text, use <a href="/components/heading">Heading</a>, <a href="/components/text">Text</a> and
    <a href="/components/link">Link</a>: they already consume the pairs and expose their own styling hooks
    (<code>--sk-heading-font-size</code>, <code>--sk-text-font-size</code>, and so on). Your own
    component reads the semantic tokens for the role it needs, always the size together with its
    line-height, and never the <code>--scale-font-*</code> primitives.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={usage} label="HTML" />
  <p>
    Every token is in the <a href="/reference">token reference</a>, with its live resolved value.
  </p>
</Base>
